# The tokeniser stage

`WeftTokeniser` is the tokeniser stage: it fills each weft's subtokens by kind. A heading gets its title, its language specifier, and its sink. Arrow and code wefts get their `::[name]` anchors; tilde, preamble, and prose wefts get their inline content and their prose anchors, read only where Markdown leaves them live. A box line gets its name and its braces. A frontmatter field gets its part, chapter, and title, or its key and value. After this stage no weft remains `incomplete`.

## The tokeniser service

Prose carries anchors too, so the tokeniser respects Markdown. An anchor inside an inline code span or inside a quote stays inert, the literal text it appears to be. An inline span lies within one line, and the prose handlers mask it there. A quote spans many lines, so the tokeniser carries a `QuoteState` from each weft to the next.

`tokeniseWefts` takes the classified wefts of a loom and gives them back tokenised, one for one. Effect's `Array.mapAccum` carries the state, and each step runs `quoteStep` and then `tokeniseWeft`. `Array.mapAccum` gives the last state beside the wefts, and `tokeniseWefts` keeps the wefts. `quoteStep` advances the state by the rules the classifier keeps. A prose line that opens a fence opens a quote, and only a line that `closesFence` accepts closes it again. Any line outside prose, a line of code or a heading, ends the quote. `quoteStep` reports `skipAnchors` on every line the quote covers, its two fences included.

`tokeniseWeft` then sends each weft to the handler of its kind, and tells the handler whether to skip the weft's anchors. It runs for every line, so it is a table of cases keyed by the weft's tag, which `byType` builds once, when the module loads. Each case receives the weft, and after it the text, the delimiters, and `skipAnchors`. A divider is whole as the classifier made it, so it passes through.

A quote never opens inside a box, because the classifier keeps a box's body free of every mark, a fence included. So the state also records whether a box is open, which `nextBox` follows line by line, and a fence in a box's body opens no quote. The anchors after that fence stay live, in the box's body and after it.

=>

export class WeftTokeniser extends Context.Service<WeftTokeniser>()('WeftTokeniser', {
  make: Effect.succeed({
    tokeniseWefts:
      (text: string, delims: AnchorDelims = defaultAnchorDelims) =>
      (wefts: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> =>
        Array.mapAccum(wefts, noQuote, (state, weft) => {
          const [next, skipAnchors] = quoteStep(state, weft)
          return [next, tokeniseWeft(weft, text, delims, skipAnchors)]
        })[1],
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make)
}

type QuoteState = {
  readonly quote: Option.Option<Fence>
  readonly inBox: boolean
}

const noQuote: QuoteState = { quote: Option.none(), inBox: false }

const isProseWeft = (weft: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  weft.type === 'PreambleWeft' || weft.type === 'TildeWeft' || weft.type === 'ProseWeft'

const quoteStep = (state: QuoteState, weft: LoomWeft): readonly [QuoteState, boolean] => {
  const inBox = nextBox(state.inBox, weft)
  return isProseWeft(weft)
    ? Option.match(state.quote, {
        onSome: (fence) => [
          { quote: closesFence(fence)(weft.source) ? Option.none() : state.quote, inBox },
          true,
        ],
        onNone: () => {
          const opened = state.inBox ? Option.none<Fence>() : openingFence(weft.source)
          return [{ quote: opened, inBox }, Option.isSome(opened)]
        },
      })
    : [{ quote: Option.none(), inBox }, false]
}

const tokeniseWeft = byType<
  LoomWeft,
  [text: string, delims: AnchorDelims, skipAnchors: boolean]
>()<LoomWeft>({
  HeadingWeft: (w, text) => tokeniseHeading(text, w),
  ArrowWeft: (w, text, delims) => tokeniseArrow(text, w, delims),
  TildeWeft: (w, text, delims, skipAnchors) =>
    tokeniseTilde(text, w, delims, skipAnchors),
  PreambleWeft: (w, text, delims, skipAnchors) =>
    tokenisePreamble(text, w, delims, skipAnchors),
  ProseWeft: (w, text, delims, skipAnchors) =>
    tokeniseProse(text, w, delims, skipAnchors),
  CodeWeft: (w, text, delims) => tokeniseCode(text, w, delims),
  FenceWeft: (w) => tokeniseFence(w),
  FrontmatterWeft: (w) => tokeniseFrontmatter(w),
  DividerWeft: (w) => w,
})

## Frontmatter fields

A frontmatter field is one `key: value` line, and `tokeniseFrontmatter` settles it by its key. It splits on the first colon and classifies what it finds.

A `Part <Roman>` line announces a numbered part, and a `Chapter <n>` line a numbered chapter, each carrying an ordinal past its keyword and a name beside it. Drop the ordinal and the membership is numberless: a bare `Part` names a part with no number, and a bare `Chapter` a chapter with no number, for a chapter a reader orders by hand rather than by position. The joined `Part <Roman>, Chapter <n>` form carries a chapter by the part it belongs to, split at the comma. A discussion reads the same way, with `Discussion` in place of `Chapter`, and a bare `Discussion` gives a discussion with no number.

A trope may declare a kind of loom of its own, and a loom of that kind names it in the same place, as `Part XI, Tasks 1` does. The tokeniser knows no trope, so `kindOf` reads any line whose key is words of letters, with a number after them or none, and which no other rule claims. The words become the `value` of a `FrontmatterKind` and the number its `number`. The row of small passes then judges the words against the kinds tropes declare.

`identityOf` reads what a loom says it is, from a key alone or from the part of a joined key that follows the comma: a chapter, a discussion, or a loom of another kind, each with the title beside it. `belongingField` reads the joined form through it and adds the part.

A trope chapter reads as a chapter too, with `Trope Chapter` in place of `Chapter`, as `Part III, Trope Chapter 4: Payments` does, and `tropeChapterField` reads it. The chapter roots a trope named by its title, so a `FrontmatterTrope` on the word `Trope` holds the title as the trope's name. A `Rooted in` line names the trope a chapter belongs to, and its value becomes a `FrontmatterTrope` too. An `Entries` line lists chapters, and `listSpans` cuts its value at each comma, so each name becomes a `FrontmatterEntry` with a span of its own, and a rename can rewrite one name alone.

A `Book` line names the book a loom belongs to, and its value becomes a `FrontmatterBook`. `Target`, `Package`, `Language`, `Status`, and `Priority` are plain keys, the last two the metadata a roadmap reads. `frontmatterFields`, from the chapter "The leaf tokens", lists them. A fence weft passes through whole, and a line that is no field settles bare, with no subtokens.

=>

const tokeniseFrontmatter = (weft: FrontmatterWeft): LoomWeft => {
  if (weft.fence !== undefined) return weft
  const lineText = weft.source
  const line = weft.position.start.line
  const base = weft.position.start.offset
  const colon = lineText.indexOf(':')
  if (colon < 0) return bareFrontmatter(weft)
  const key = trimSpan(lineText.slice(0, colon), base)
  const value = trimSpan(lineText.slice(colon + 1), base + colon + 1)
  return buildFrontmatterField(weft, line, key, value)
}

const buildFrontmatterField = (
  weft: FrontmatterWeft,
  line: number,
  key: TrimmedSpan,
  value: TrimmedSpan,
): FrontmatterWeft => {
  if (/(?:^|,\s*)Trope Chapter(?:\s|$)/.test(key.value))
    return tropeChapterField(weft, line, key, value)
  const belonging = belongingField(weft, line, key, value)
  if (Option.isSome(belonging)) return belonging.value
  if (key.value.startsWith('Part '))
    return nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
      position: weft.position,
      source: weft.source,
      health: okHealth,
      part: ordinalToken(FrontmatterPartTokenSchema, line, key, 'Part '),
      partName: fieldToken(FrontmatterPartNameTokenSchema, line, value),
    })
  if (key.value === 'Part')
    return nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
      position: weft.position,
      source: weft.source,
      health: okHealth,
      partName: fieldToken(FrontmatterPartNameTokenSchema, line, value),
    })
  if (key.value === 'Rooted in')
    return nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
      position: weft.position,
      source: weft.source,
      health: okHealth,
      rootedIn: fieldToken(FrontmatterTropeTokenSchema, line, value),
    })
  if (key.value === 'Entries')
    return nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
      position: weft.position,
      source: weft.source,
      health: okHealth,
      entries: listSpans(value).map((name) => fieldToken(FrontmatterEntryTokenSchema, line, name)),
    })
  if (key.value === 'Book')
    return nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
      position: weft.position,
      source: weft.source,
      health: okHealth,
      book: fieldToken(FrontmatterBookTokenSchema, line, value),
    })
  if (frontmatterFields.includes(key.value))
    return nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
      position: weft.position,
      source: weft.source,
      health: okHealth,
      key: fieldToken(FrontmatterKeyTokenSchema, line, key),
      value: fieldToken(FrontmatterValueTokenSchema, line, value),
    })
  return Option.match(identityOf(line, key, value), {
    onNone: () => bareFrontmatter(weft),
    onSome: (identity) =>
      nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
        position: weft.position,
        source: weft.source,
        health: okHealth,
        ...identity,
      }),
  })
}

type Identity = Pick<FrontmatterWeft, 'chapter' | 'discussion' | 'kind' | 'title'>

const kindLine = /^([A-Za-z]+(?: [A-Za-z]+)*)(?: +(\d\S*))?$/

const kindOf = (line: number, own: TrimmedSpan): Option.Option<FrontmatterKindToken> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(kindLine.exec(own.value)),
    Option.filter(([, words]) => words !== 'Part' && !frontmatterKeys.includes(words ?? '')),
    Option.map(([, words, number]) =>
      nodeOf(FrontmatterKindTokenSchema, {
        position: span(line, own.start, own.end),
        source: own.value,
        health: okHealth,
        value: words ?? '',
        number: number ?? '',
      }),
    ),
  )

const identityOf = (line: number, own: TrimmedSpan, value: TrimmedSpan): Option.Option<Identity> => {
  const title = fieldToken(FrontmatterTitleTokenSchema, line, value)
  if (own.value === 'Chapter') return Option.some({ title })
  if (own.value.startsWith('Chapter '))
    return Option.some({
      chapter: ordinalToken(FrontmatterChapterTokenSchema, line, own, 'Chapter '),
      title,
    })
  if (own.value === 'Discussion' || own.value.startsWith('Discussion '))
    return Option.some({
      discussion: ordinalToken(FrontmatterDiscussionTokenSchema, line, own, 'Discussion'),
      title,
    })
  return Option.map(kindOf(line, own), (kind) => ({ kind, title }))
}

const tropeChapterField = (
  weft: FrontmatterWeft,
  line: number,
  key: TrimmedSpan,
  value: TrimmedSpan,
): FrontmatterWeft => {
  const comma = key.value.indexOf(',')
  const own = comma < 0 ? key : trimSpan(key.value.slice(comma + 1), key.start + comma + 1)
  const mark = trimSpan(own.value.slice(0, 'Trope'.length), own.start)
  const chapterSpan = trimSpan(own.value.slice('Trope'.length), own.start + 'Trope'.length)
  return nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
    position: weft.position,
    source: weft.source,
    health: okHealth,
    ...(comma < 0
      ? {}
      : {
          part: ordinalToken(
            FrontmatterPartTokenSchema,
            line,
            trimSpan(key.value.slice(0, comma), key.start),
            'Part ',
          ),
        }),
    ...(chapterSpan.value === 'Chapter'
      ? {}
      : { chapter: ordinalToken(FrontmatterChapterTokenSchema, line, chapterSpan, 'Chapter ') }),
    title: fieldToken(FrontmatterTitleTokenSchema, line, value),
    trope: nodeOf(FrontmatterTropeTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, mark.start, mark.end),
      source: mark.value,
      health: okHealth,
      value: value.value,
    }),
  })
}

const belongingField = (
  weft: FrontmatterWeft,
  line: number,
  key: TrimmedSpan,
  value: TrimmedSpan,
): Option.Option<FrontmatterWeft> => {
  const comma = key.value.indexOf(',')
  if (!key.value.startsWith('Part ') || comma < 0) return Option.none()
  const partSpan = trimSpan(key.value.slice(0, comma), key.start)
  const own = trimSpan(key.value.slice(comma + 1), key.start + comma + 1)
  return Option.map(identityOf(line, own, value), (identity) =>
    nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
      position: weft.position,
      source: weft.source,
      health: okHealth,
      part: ordinalToken(FrontmatterPartTokenSchema, line, partSpan, 'Part '),
      ...identity,
    }),
  )
}

~

Three builders shape the tokens. `ordinalToken` reads a `Part III` or `Chapter 1` marker, its value the ordinal past the keyword. `fieldToken` builds a plain-valued token — a name, a title, a key, or a path. `bareFrontmatter` is the fence's passthrough and the fallback for a line that is no field: a weft with no subtokens.

Most tokens share one of two shapes, and a builder that serves many kinds of token is written against the shape. A `Token` is a node with no field of its own: its tag and the position, source and health every node carries. A `ValueToken` adds the one field most tokens have, the `value` they hold. Each takes the token's tag as a parameter, so a builder that is handed a schema gives back that schema's own token.

=>

type TrimmedSpan = {
  readonly value: string
  readonly start: number
  readonly end: number
}

type Token<Tag extends string> = {
  readonly type: Tag
  readonly position: Position
  readonly source: string
  readonly health: Health
}

type ValueToken<Tag extends string, Value extends string = string> = Token<Tag> & {
  readonly value: Value
}

const ordinalToken = <Tag extends string>(
  schema: NodeSchema<ValueToken<Tag>>,
  line: number,
  key: TrimmedSpan,
  keyword: string,
): ValueToken<Tag> =>
  nodeOf(schema, {
    position: span(line, key.start, key.end),
    source: key.value,
    health: okHealth,
    value: key.value.slice(keyword.length).trim(),
  })

const fieldToken = <Tag extends string>(
  schema: NodeSchema<ValueToken<Tag>>,
  line: number,
  seg: TrimmedSpan,
): ValueToken<Tag> =>
  nodeOf(schema, {
    position: span(line, seg.start, seg.end),
    source: seg.value,
    health: okHealth,
    value: seg.value,
  })

const listSpans = (value: TrimmedSpan): ReadonlyArray<TrimmedSpan> =>
  Array.mapAccum(value.value.split(','), value.start, (at, part) => [
    at + part.length + 1,
    trimSpan(part, at),
  ])[1].filter((name) => name.value !== '')

const bareFrontmatter = (weft: FrontmatterWeft): FrontmatterWeft =>
  nodeOf(FrontmatterWeftSchema, {
    position: weft.position,
    source: weft.source,
    health: okHealth,
  })

## Body wefts

These handlers fill the body wefts. `inlineAfter` pulls a line's inline content after its marker — the code after `=>`, the prose after `~`. `tokeniseArrow` and `tokeniseCode` scan a code line for its `::[name]` anchors. `tokeniseTilde`, `tokenisePreamble`, and `tokeniseProse` scan a prose line for its anchors, and the preamble for its warps too. `maskInlineCode` blanks an inline code span before the scan, and `blankIfSkipped` blanks the whole line inside a fence, so a token survives only where Markdown leaves it live. Each aggregates its subtokens' health into the weft. A preamble or prose weft that carries a box is a line of colons, not of prose, so `tokenisePreamble` and `tokeniseProse` hand it to `tokeniseBoxLine`, which the section "A box line" describes.

=>

const codeProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(CodeTokenSchema))
const proseProbe = Option.getOrThrow(getProbe(ProseTokenSchema))

const inlineAfter = <Tag extends string>(
  schema: NodeSchema<Token<Tag>>,
  probe: RegExp,
  text: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): Option.Option<Token<Tag>> => {
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  const lineText = text.slice(lineStart, linePosition.end.offset).replace(/\r?\n$/, '')
  return pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(probe.exec(lineText)),
    Option.filter((m): m is RegExpExecArray & { index: number } => m.index !== undefined),
    Option.map((m) =>
      nodeOf(schema, {
        position: span(
          linePosition.start.line,
          lineStart + m.index,
          lineStart + m.index + m[0].length,
        ),
        source: m[0],
        health: okHealth,
      }),
    ),
  )
}

const lineSlice = (text: string, position: Position): string =>
  text.slice(position.start.offset, position.end.offset)

const maskInlineCode = (line: string): string =>
  line.replace(/`[^`]*`/g, (m) => ' '.repeat(m.length))

const blankIfSkipped = (line: string, skip: boolean): string =>
  skip ? ' '.repeat(line.length) : maskInlineCode(line)

const tokeniseArrow = (text: string, weft: ArrowWeft, delims: AnchorDelims): LoomWeft => {
  const code = inlineAfter(CodeTokenSchema, codeProbe, text, weft.position)
  const { tokens: anchors, unexpected } = constructAnchors(
    lineSlice(text, weft.position),
    weft.position,
    delims,
  )
  const status = aggregateStatus([
    weft.arrow.health.status,
    ...Option.toArray(code).map((c) => c.health.status),
    ...anchors.map((a) => a.health.status),
    ...unexpected.map(() => 'error' as const),
  ])
  return nodeOf(ArrowWeftSchema, {
    position: weft.position,
    source: lineSlice(text, weft.position),
    health: strayHealth(text, status, unexpected),
    unexpected: unexpected.length > 0 ? unexpected : undefined,
    arrow: weft.arrow,
    code: Option.getOrUndefined(code),
    anchors,
  })
}

const tokeniseTilde = (
  text: string,
  weft: TildeWeft,
  delims: AnchorDelims,
  skipAnchors: boolean,
): LoomWeft => {
  const lineText = lineSlice(text, weft.position)
  const prose = inlineAfter(ProseTokenSchema, proseProbe, text, weft.position)
  const { tokens: anchors, unexpected } = constructAnchors(
    lineText,
    weft.position,
    delims,
    blankIfSkipped(lineText, skipAnchors),
  )
  const status = aggregateStatus([
    weft.tilde.health.status,
    ...Option.toArray(prose).map((p) => p.health.status),
    ...anchors.map((a) => a.health.status),
    ...unexpected.map(() => 'error' as const),
  ])
  return nodeOf(TildeWeftSchema, {
    position: weft.position,
    source: lineText,
    health: strayHealth(text, status, unexpected),
    unexpected: unexpected.length > 0 ? unexpected : undefined,
    tilde: weft.tilde,
    prose: Option.getOrUndefined(prose),
    anchors,
  })
}

const tokenisePreamble = (
  text: string,
  weft: PreambleWeft,
  delims: AnchorDelims,
  skipAnchors: boolean,
): LoomWeft =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(weft.box), {
    onSome: (box) => tokeniseBoxLine(text, weft, box),
    onNone: () => tokenisePreambleLine(text, weft, delims, skipAnchors),
  })

const tokenisePreambleLine = (
  text: string,
  weft: PreambleWeft,
  delims: AnchorDelims,
  skipAnchors: boolean,
): LoomWeft => {
  const lineText = lineSlice(text, weft.position)
  const scanText = blankIfSkipped(lineText, skipAnchors)
  const { tokens: warps, unexpected: warpStray } = constructWarps(lineText, weft.position, scanText)
  const { tokens: anchors, unexpected: anchorStray } = constructAnchors(
    lineText,
    weft.position,
    delims,
    scanText,
  )
  const unexpected = [...warpStray, ...anchorStray]
  const status = aggregateStatus([
    ...warps.map((w) => w.health.status),
    ...anchors.map((a) => a.health.status),
    ...unexpected.map(() => 'error' as const),
  ])
  return nodeOf(PreambleWeftSchema, {
    position: weft.position,
    source: lineText,
    health: strayHealth(text, status, unexpected),
    unexpected: unexpected.length > 0 ? unexpected : undefined,
    warps,
    anchors,
  })
}

const tokeniseProse = (
  text: string,
  weft: ProseWeft,
  delims: AnchorDelims,
  skipAnchors: boolean,
): LoomWeft =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(weft.box), {
    onSome: (box) => tokeniseBoxLine(text, weft, box),
    onNone: () => tokeniseProseLine(text, weft, delims, skipAnchors),
  })

const tokeniseProseLine = (
  text: string,
  weft: ProseWeft,
  delims: AnchorDelims,
  skipAnchors: boolean,
): LoomWeft => {
  const lineText = lineSlice(text, weft.position)
  const { tokens: anchors, unexpected } = constructAnchors(
    lineText,
    weft.position,
    delims,
    blankIfSkipped(lineText, skipAnchors),
  )
  const status = aggregateStatus([
    ...anchors.map((a) => a.health.status),
    ...unexpected.map(() => 'error' as const),
  ])
  return nodeOf(ProseWeftSchema, {
    position: weft.position,
    source: lineText,
    health: strayHealth(text, status, unexpected),
    unexpected: unexpected.length > 0 ? unexpected : undefined,
    anchors,
  })
}

const tokeniseCode = (text: string, weft: CodeWeft, delims: AnchorDelims): LoomWeft => {
  const { tokens: anchors, unexpected } = constructAnchors(
    lineSlice(text, weft.position),
    weft.position,
    delims,
  )
  const status = aggregateStatus([
    ...anchors.map((a) => a.health.status),
    ...unexpected.map(() => 'error' as const),
  ])
  return nodeOf(CodeWeftSchema, {
    position: weft.position,
    source: lineSlice(text, weft.position),
    health: strayHealth(text, status, unexpected),
    unexpected: unexpected.length > 0 ? unexpected : undefined,
    anchors,
  })
}

~

A fence line around fenced code has little left to settle. The classifier has already found its backticks and its side. `tokeniseFence` reads an opening fence's label, the text after the backticks, trimmed, when there is any. A closing fence carries nothing after its backticks, so it is whole as it stands.

=>

const tokeniseFence = (weft: FenceWeft): LoomWeft => {
  const lineStart = weft.position.start.offset
  const after = weft.fence.position.end.offset
  const rest = weft.source.replace(/\r?\n$/, '').slice(after - lineStart)
  const name = rest.trim()
  const start = after + rest.length - rest.trimStart().length
  const label =
    weft.side === 'open' && name !== ''
      ? nodeOf(FenceLabelTokenSchema, {
          position: span(weft.position.start.line, start, start + name.length),
          source: name,
          health: okHealth,
        })
      : undefined
  return nodeOf(FenceWeftSchema, { ...weft, health: okHealth, label })
}

## Health and positions

`aggregateStatus` folds a list of statuses up the lattice ok < incomplete < warning < error, the worst winning. `span` builds a position; `contentEnd` is the offset where a line's content ends, before its newline terminator, and is the bound every construct slices and positions against, so the EOL never leaks into a label, an anchor, or a diagnostic; `missingClosing` is the error health for an unclosed bracket, its diagnostic spanning the open delimiter to that content end so the squiggle lands on the construct rather than the gap after it. `brokenLabel` is the error health for a label the schema rejected. It raises an `EmptyLabel` or a `MalformedLabel` fault through the catalog, keyed by construct, so the same wording reaches the editor wherever a label is built.

A weft that kept stray fragments takes its health from `strayHealth`. A stray is a fragment the line had no place for: a second specifier on a heading, an orphan bracket. Its status already fails the weft, but a status alone tells nobody anything. `strayHealth` gives each stray a `StrayFragment` diagnostic at its own position, so the tangle refuses the file and the editor underlines the stray itself.

Pairing reports a stray by its delimiters, so a second `{Tangle}` arrives as an orphan `{` and an orphan `}`. A diagnostic that named the two braces apart would make a reader assemble the mistake. `strayFragments` stitches an orphan opener and the closer that matches it back into the one fragment the author wrote, and the diagnostic quotes `{Tangle}` whole.

=>

const statusRank: Record<Health['status'], number> = {
  ok: 0,
  incomplete: 1,
  warning: 2,
  error: 3,
}

const joinStatus = (a: Health['status'], b: Health['status']): Health['status'] =>
  statusRank[a] >= statusRank[b] ? a : b

const aggregateStatus = (statuses: ReadonlyArray<Health['status']>): Health['status'] =>
  statuses.reduce(joinStatus, 'ok' as Health['status'])

const span = (line: number, start: number, end: number): Position => ({
  start: { line, offset: start },
  end: { line, offset: end },
})

const contentEnd = (lineText: string, lineStart: number): number =>
  lineStart + lineText.replace(/\r?\n$/, '').length

const missingClosing = (line: number, from: number, lineEnd: number, expected: string): Health =>
  faulty(UnclosedDelimiter({ expected }), span(line, from, lineEnd))

type Fragment = { readonly value: string; readonly position: Position }

const closerOf: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = { '{': '}', '[': ']' }

const closes = (opener: Fragment, stray: UnexpectedToken): boolean =>
  closerOf[opener.value] === stray.value && opener.position.start.line === stray.position.start.line

const strayFragments = (
  text: string,
  unexpected: ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken>,
): ReadonlyArray<Fragment> =>
  unexpected.reduce(
    (fragments, stray) =>
      Option.match(
        Option.filter(Array.last(fragments), (last) => closes(last, stray)),
        {
          onNone: () => [...fragments, { value: stray.value, position: stray.position }],
          onSome: (opener) => [
            ...Array.dropRight(fragments, 1),
            {
              value: text.slice(opener.position.start.offset, stray.position.end.offset),
              position: { start: opener.position.start, end: stray.position.end },
            },
          ],
        },
      ),
    [] as ReadonlyArray<Fragment>,
  )

const strayHealth = (
  text: string,
  status: Health['status'],
  unexpected: ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken>,
): Health => ({
  status,
  diagnostics: strayFragments(text, unexpected).flatMap(
    (fragment) => faulty(StrayFragment({ value: fragment.value }), fragment.position).diagnostics,
  ),
})

const brokenLabel = (construct: MalformedConstruct, value: string, position: Position): Health =>
  faulty(value === '' ? EmptyLabel({ construct }) : MalformedLabel({ construct, value }), position)

## Label builders

A label that fails its strict pattern must still be kept. `buildSpecifierLabel` and `buildSinkFileLabel` each try the schema, and on rejection build a valid token with an empty value, the error health `brokenLabel` renders, and the rejected text stashed in `unexpected` — strict validation stays on, and bytes are never dropped. The sink builder reuses the `path` construct for its broken health, since a malformed file name is a malformed path.

=>

const decodeSpecifierLabel = Schema.decodeUnknownResult(SpecifierLabelTokenSchema)
const decodeSinkFileLabel = Schema.decodeUnknownResult(SinkFileLabelTokenSchema)

const buildSpecifierLabel = (value: string, position: Position): SpecifierLabelToken =>
  pipe(
    decodeSpecifierLabel({
      type: 'SpecifierLabel',
      position,
      source: value,
      health: okHealth,
      value,
    }),
    Result.getOrElse(() =>
      nodeOf(SpecifierLabelTokenSchema, {
        position,
        source: value,
        health: brokenLabel('specifier', value, position),
        value: '',
        unexpected: [nodeOf(UnexpectedTokenSchema, { position, value })],
      }),
    ),
  )

const buildSinkFileLabel = (value: string, position: Position): SinkFileLabelToken =>
  pipe(
    decodeSinkFileLabel({
      type: 'SinkFileLabel',
      position,
      source: value,
      health: okHealth,
      value,
    }),
    Result.getOrElse(() =>
      nodeOf(SinkFileLabelTokenSchema, {
        position,
        source: value,
        health: brokenLabel('path', value, position),
        value: '',
        unexpected: [nodeOf(UnexpectedTokenSchema, { position, value })],
      }),
    ),
  )

## Scanners

`makeScanner` reads a token schema's `Probe` once and returns a per-line scanner that emits a typed token at every match, with real positions and ok health. The scanners for the specifier and sink delimiters are built from it. Each of those schemas names the one text its token holds, such as `{`, and its probe matches that text and nothing else, so the scanner gives the match as the token's value. A `Scannable` is a schema a scanner can be made from: one that carries a probe, and that `nodeOf` can construct a `ValueToken` of.

=>

type Scannable<Tag extends string, Value extends string> = NodeSchema<ValueToken<Tag, Value>> &
  Schema.Schema<ValueToken<Tag, Value>>

type Scanner<T> = (lineText: string, linePosition: Position) => ReadonlyArray<T>

const makeScanner = <Tag extends string, Value extends string>(
  schema: Scannable<Tag, Value>,
): Scanner<ValueToken<Tag, Value>> => {
  const probe = Option.getOrThrowWith(
    getProbe(schema),
    () => new Error('makeScanner: schema has no Probe annotation'),
  )
  return (lineText, linePosition) => {
    const line = linePosition.start.line
    const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
    return [...lineText.matchAll(probe)]
      .filter((match) => match.index !== undefined)
      .map((match) => {
        const i = match.index!
        return nodeOf(schema, {
          position: span(line, lineStart + i, lineStart + i + match[0].length),
          source: match[0],
          health: okHealth,
          value: match[0] as Value,
        })
      })
  }
}

const scanSpecifierOpen = makeScanner(SpecifierOpenTokenSchema)
const scanSpecifierClose = makeScanner(SpecifierCloseTokenSchema)
const scanSinkOpen = makeScanner(SinkOpenTokenSchema)
const scanSinkClose = makeScanner(SinkCloseTokenSchema)

## Pairing specifiers

Each scanner finds opens and closes independently; construction pairs them. `partitionFirstClose` matches an open with the first close after it. `constructSpecifier` pairs `{` and `}` into a language label. `constructSink` pairs `[` and `]` into a sink, its inner text the file a `{Tangle}` section emits. When no close follows, each synthesises one at the line's content end — the offset before the newline — so a missing bracket reports once, as `expected closing`, and the captured label never swallows the line's terminator.

=>

type Construction<T> = {
  readonly token: Option.Option<T>
  readonly unexpected: ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken>
}

type Positioned = { readonly position: Position; readonly value: string }

const toUnexpected = (t: Positioned): UnexpectedToken =>
  nodeOf(UnexpectedTokenSchema, { position: t.position, value: t.value })

const partitionFirstClose = <C extends { position: Position }>(
  open: { position: Position },
  closes: ReadonlyArray<C>,
): { match: C | null; rest: ReadonlyArray<C> } => {
  const matchIdx = closes.findIndex((c) => c.position.start.offset > open.position.start.offset)
  return matchIdx < 0
    ? { match: null, rest: closes }
    : { match: closes[matchIdx], rest: closes.filter((_, i) => i !== matchIdx) }
}

const buildLabelSpecifier = (
  open: SpecifierOpenToken,
  close: SpecifierCloseToken,
  labelText: string,
  labelPos: Position,
  specifierPos: Position,
  specifierSource: string,
): SpecifierToken => {
  const label = buildSpecifierLabel(labelText, labelPos)
  const status = aggregateStatus([open.health.status, label.health.status, close.health.status])
  return nodeOf(SpecifierTokenSchema, {
    position: specifierPos,
    source: specifierSource,
    health: { status, diagnostics: [] },
    open,
    label,
    close,
  })
}

const constructSpecifier = (
  opens: ReadonlyArray<SpecifierOpenToken>,
  closes: ReadonlyArray<SpecifierCloseToken>,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): Construction<SpecifierToken> => {
  if (opens.length === 0) {
    return {
      token: Option.none(),
      unexpected: closes.map(toUnexpected),
    }
  }

  const line = linePosition.start.line
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  const lineEnd = contentEnd(lineText, lineStart)

  const [open, ...extraOpens] = opens
  const { match, rest: extraCloses } = partitionFirstClose(open, closes)

  const close: SpecifierCloseToken =
    match ??
    nodeOf(SpecifierCloseTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, lineEnd, lineEnd),
      source: '',
      health: missingClosing(line, open.position.start.offset, lineEnd, '}'),
      value: '}',
    })

  const labelStart = open.position.end.offset
  const labelEnd = match ? match.position.start.offset : lineEnd
  const labelText = lineText.slice(labelStart - lineStart, labelEnd - lineStart)
  const labelPos = span(line, labelStart, labelEnd)
  const specifierPos = span(line, open.position.start.offset, close.position.end.offset)
  const specifierSource = lineText.slice(
    specifierPos.start.offset - lineStart,
    specifierPos.end.offset - lineStart,
  )

  return {
    token: Option.some(
      buildLabelSpecifier(open, close, labelText, labelPos, specifierPos, specifierSource),
    ),
    unexpected: [...extraOpens.map(toUnexpected), ...extraCloses.map(toUnexpected)],
  }
}

const constructSink = (
  opens: ReadonlyArray<SinkOpenToken>,
  closes: ReadonlyArray<SinkCloseToken>,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): Construction<SinkToken> => {
  if (opens.length === 0) {
    return {
      token: Option.none(),
      unexpected: closes.map(toUnexpected),
    }
  }

  const line = linePosition.start.line
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  const lineEnd = contentEnd(lineText, lineStart)

  const [open, ...extraOpens] = opens
  const { match, rest: extraCloses } = partitionFirstClose(open, closes)

  const close: SinkCloseToken =
    match ??
    nodeOf(SinkCloseTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, lineEnd, lineEnd),
      source: '',
      health: missingClosing(line, open.position.start.offset, lineEnd, ']'),
      value: ']',
    })

  const innerStart = open.position.end.offset
  const innerEnd = match ? match.position.start.offset : lineEnd
  const inner = lineText.slice(innerStart - lineStart, innerEnd - lineStart)
  const sinkPos = span(line, open.position.start.offset, close.position.end.offset)
  const sinkSource = lineText.slice(
    sinkPos.start.offset - lineStart,
    sinkPos.end.offset - lineStart,
  )

  const fileTrim = trimSpan(inner, innerStart)
  const file = buildSinkFileLabel(fileTrim.value, span(line, fileTrim.start, fileTrim.end))

  const status = aggregateStatus([open.health.status, file.health.status, close.health.status])

  return {
    token: Option.some(
      nodeOf(SinkTokenSchema, {
        position: sinkPos,
        source: sinkSource,
        health: { status, diagnostics: [] },
        open,
        file,
        close,
      }),
    ),
    unexpected: [...extraOpens.map(toUnexpected), ...extraCloses.map(toUnexpected)],
  }
}

## Pairing warp delimiters

Warps and anchors no longer share delimiters: a warp is `{{…}}` in a preamble line, an anchor is `::[…]` in a code line. `pairWarpDelims` pairs a preamble line's `{{` and `}}` greedily left to right — pairing an unmatched open with a synthetic `}}` at end-of-line, and surfacing unmatched closes as stray `unexpected` tokens. Anchors pair differently: `scanAnchorOpen` finds the `::[` opens, and each closes on the first `]` after it, found directly rather than scanned, because `]` is ordinary in product code — only the one closing an open is structural, and a bare `]` is never a stray.

=>

const scanWarpOpen = makeScanner(WarpOpenTokenSchema)
const scanWarpClose = makeScanner(WarpCloseTokenSchema)

type WarpPair = { readonly open: WarpOpenToken; readonly close: WarpCloseToken }

type PairAcc = {
  readonly pairs: ReadonlyArray<WarpPair>
  readonly remaining: ReadonlyArray<WarpCloseToken>
}

const pairWarpDelims = (
  opens: ReadonlyArray<WarpOpenToken>,
  closes: ReadonlyArray<WarpCloseToken>,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): {
  readonly pairs: ReadonlyArray<WarpPair>
  readonly stray: ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken>
} => {
  const line = linePosition.start.line
  const lineEnd = contentEnd(lineText, linePosition.start.offset)
  const synthClose = (open: WarpOpenToken): WarpCloseToken =>
    nodeOf(WarpCloseTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, lineEnd, lineEnd),
      source: '',
      health: missingClosing(line, open.position.start.offset, lineEnd, '}}'),
      value: '}}',
    })

  const { pairs, remaining } = opens.reduce<PairAcc>(
    (acc, open) => {
      const idx = acc.remaining.findIndex(
        (c) => c.position.start.offset > open.position.start.offset,
      )
      return idx < 0
        ? {
            pairs: [...acc.pairs, { open, close: synthClose(open) }],
            remaining: acc.remaining,
          }
        : {
            pairs: [...acc.pairs, { open, close: acc.remaining[idx] }],
            remaining: acc.remaining.filter((_, i) => i !== idx),
          }
    },
    { pairs: [], remaining: closes },
  )

  return { pairs, stray: remaining.map(toUnexpected) }
}

## Warp content

A warp's content is `name [: type] = value`. `sliceTop` cuts at the first top-level `,` or `;`, tracking bracket depth, since a warp declares one binding; `trimSpan` trims whitespace and reports the trimmed span. `buildWarpName` mirrors the label builders. `buildOpaqueSegment` produces the opaque annotation and default tokens. It takes the schema of the token it is to construct, flags an empty value, and returns any trailing content as `unexpected`.

=>

type SliceAcc = { readonly depth: number; readonly cut: Option.Option<number> }

const stepSlice = (s: SliceAcc, c: string, i: number): SliceAcc => {
  if (Option.isSome(s.cut)) return s
  if (c === '<' || c === '(' || c === '[') return { depth: s.depth + 1, cut: s.cut }
  if (c === '>' || c === ')' || c === ']') return { depth: Math.max(0, s.depth - 1), cut: s.cut }
  if ((c === ',' || c === ';') && s.depth === 0) return { depth: s.depth, cut: Option.some(i) }
  return s
}

const sliceTop = (raw: string): { kept: string; extraStart: number } => {
  const final = [...raw].reduce<SliceAcc>(stepSlice, {
    depth: 0,
    cut: Option.none(),
  })
  return Option.match(final.cut, {
    onNone: () => ({ kept: raw, extraStart: raw.length }),
    onSome: (idx) => ({ kept: raw.slice(0, idx), extraStart: idx }),
  })
}

const trimSpan = (raw: string, rawStart: number): { value: string; start: number; end: number } => {
  const left = raw.match(/^\s*/)?.[0].length ?? 0
  const right = raw.match(/\s*$/)?.[0].length ?? 0
  return {
    value: raw.slice(left, raw.length - right),
    start: rawStart + left,
    end: rawStart + raw.length - right,
  }
}

const decodeWarpName = Schema.decodeUnknownResult(WarpNameTokenSchema)

const buildWarpName = (value: string, position: Position): WarpNameToken =>
  pipe(
    decodeWarpName({
      type: 'WarpName',
      position,
      source: value,
      health: okHealth,
      value,
    }),
    Result.getOrElse(() =>
      nodeOf(WarpNameTokenSchema, {
        position,
        source: value,
        health: brokenLabel('warpName', value, position),
        value: '',
        unexpected: [nodeOf(UnexpectedTokenSchema, { position, value })],
      }),
    ),
  )

const buildOpaqueSegment = <Tag extends string>(
  schema: NodeSchema<ValueToken<Tag>>,
  construct: EmptyConstruct,
  raw: string,
  rawStart: number,
  line: number,
): { token: ValueToken<Tag>; extras: ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken> } => {
  const { kept, extraStart } = sliceTop(raw)
  const { value, start, end } = trimSpan(kept, rawStart)

  const tokenPos = span(line, start, end)
  const health: Health = value === '' ? faulty(EmptyLabel({ construct }), tokenPos) : okHealth

  const token = nodeOf(schema, {
    position: tokenPos,
    source: value,
    health,
    value,
  })

  const extras: UnexpectedToken[] =
    extraStart < raw.length
      ? [
          nodeOf(UnexpectedTokenSchema, {
            position: span(line, rawStart + extraStart, rawStart + raw.length),
            value: raw.slice(extraStart),
          }),
        ]
      : []

  return { token, extras }
}

const buildWarpAnnotation = (raw: string, rawStart: number, line: number) =>
  buildOpaqueSegment(WarpAnnotationTokenSchema, 'warpAnnotation', raw, rawStart, line)

const buildWarpDefault = (raw: string, rawStart: number, line: number) =>
  buildOpaqueSegment(WarpDefaultTokenSchema, 'warpDefault', raw, rawStart, line)

## Building a warp

`buildWarp` reads a warp's content as `name [: type] = value`. It splits on the binding `=` first — the value is what a Warp binds — then splits the part before it on `:` to separate the name from an optional type. `findAssign` finds that binding `=`: the first one that is a plain assignment, never part of `=>`, `==`, `>=`, `<=`, or `!=`, so a value that holds those operators is read whole. A Warp with no value binds nothing, so `buildWarp` faults it with `MissingWarpValue`. `splitDecl` separates the name from its optional type, and `assembleWarp` gathers the parts, aggregates their health, and carries the missing-value fault and any extras.

=>

const findAssign = (s: string): Option.Option<number> => {
  const isCompound = (i: number): boolean => {
    const prev = s[i - 1]
    const next = s[i + 1]
    return (
      prev === '=' || prev === '!' || prev === '<' || prev === '>' || next === '=' || next === '>'
    )
  }
  const idx = [...s].findIndex((c, i) => c === '=' && !isCompound(i))
  return idx < 0 ? Option.none() : Option.some(idx)
}

const findChar = (s: string, c: string): Option.Option<number> => {
  const idx = s.indexOf(c)
  return idx < 0 ? Option.none() : Option.some(idx)
}

interface WarpDecl {
  readonly name: WarpNameToken
  readonly annotation: WarpAnnotationToken | undefined
  readonly extras: ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken>
}

const splitDecl = (decl: string, declStart: number, line: number): WarpDecl =>
  Option.match(findChar(decl, ':'), {
    onNone: () => {
      const n = trimSpan(decl, declStart)
      return {
        name: buildWarpName(n.value, span(line, n.start, n.end)),
        annotation: undefined,
        extras: [],
      }
    },
    onSome: (colonIdx) => {
      const n = trimSpan(decl.slice(0, colonIdx), declStart)
      const { token: annotation, extras } = buildWarpAnnotation(
        decl.slice(colonIdx + 1),
        declStart + colonIdx + 1,
        line,
      )
      return {
        name: buildWarpName(n.value, span(line, n.start, n.end)),
        annotation,
        extras,
      }
    },
  })

const buildWarp = (
  open: WarpOpenToken,
  close: WarpCloseToken,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): WarpToken => {
  const line = linePosition.start.line
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  const contentStart = open.position.end.offset
  const contentEnd = close.position.start.offset
  const content = lineText.slice(contentStart - lineStart, contentEnd - lineStart)
  const warpSource = lineText.slice(
    open.position.start.offset - lineStart,
    close.position.end.offset - lineStart,
  )
  const warpPos = span(line, open.position.start.offset, close.position.end.offset)

  return Option.match(findAssign(content), {
    onSome: (eqIdx) => {
      const { name, annotation, extras } = splitDecl(content.slice(0, eqIdx), contentStart, line)
      const { token: defaultToken, extras: defExtras } = buildWarpDefault(
        content.slice(eqIdx + 1),
        contentStart + eqIdx + 1,
        line,
      )
      return assembleWarp(
        open,
        close,
        name,
        annotation,
        defaultToken,
        [...extras, ...defExtras],
        line,
        warpSource,
      )
    },
    onNone: () => {
      const { name, annotation, extras } = splitDecl(content, contentStart, line)
      const own =
        name.health.status === 'ok'
          ? faulty(MissingWarpValue({ name: name.value }), warpPos)
          : okHealth
      return assembleWarp(open, close, name, annotation, undefined, extras, line, warpSource, own)
    },
  })
}

const assembleWarp = (
  open: WarpOpenToken,
  close: WarpCloseToken,
  name: WarpNameToken,
  annotation: WarpAnnotationToken | undefined,
  defaultToken: WarpDefaultToken | undefined,
  extras: ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken>,
  line: number,
  source: string,
  own: Health = okHealth,
): WarpToken => {
  const subStatuses = [
    open.health.status,
    name.health.status,
    ...(annotation ? [annotation.health.status] : []),
    ...(defaultToken ? [defaultToken.health.status] : []),
    close.health.status,
  ]
  const status = aggregateStatus([
    ...subStatuses,
    ...extras.map(() => 'error' as const),
    own.status,
  ])
  return nodeOf(WarpTokenSchema, {
    position: span(line, open.position.start.offset, close.position.end.offset),
    source,
    health: { status, diagnostics: own.diagnostics },
    unexpected: extras.length > 0 ? extras : undefined,
    open,
    name,
    annotation,
    default: defaultToken,
    close,
  })
}

## Building an anchor

`buildWarpAnchor` reads a `::[…]` reference's content as its name — an identifier or a multi-word heading name. Content the anchor pattern rejects (empty) yields an error-health name and surfaces the text as `unexpected`. `anchorTargetAt` reads an optional target in parentheses right after the close. The target is opaque here. The product pass reads it as the file a module anchor names, and faults it on an anchor that composes, as the chapter "Referring to modules" describes. An open paren with no close stays product code. `anchorSpecifierAt` reads the braces that follow the anchor, right after its target when it has one, and otherwise right after its close, through `bracesAt`, which the next section describes. An open brace with no close stays product code, never a broken specifier. An anchor takes no sink, so square brackets after it stay code, or prose, as in `items[0]`. `assembleAnchor` gathers the parts and stretches the anchor's span across whichever trails it.

=>

const unexpectedIfNonEmpty = (position: Position, value: string): ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken> =>
  value.length > 0 ? [nodeOf(UnexpectedTokenSchema, { position, value })] : []

const decodeWarpAnchorName = Schema.decodeUnknownResult(WarpAnchorNameTokenSchema)

type Braces = SpecifierToken | ParametersToken

const anchorSpecifierAt = (
  openAt: number,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): Option.Option<{ specifier: Braces; end: number }> =>
  lineText[openAt - linePosition.start.offset] === '{'
    ? bracesAt(openAt, lineText, linePosition)
    : Option.none()

const anchorTargetAt = (
  close: AnchorCloseToken,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): Option.Option<{ target: WarpAnchorTargetToken; end: number }> => {
  const line = linePosition.start.line
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  const openAt = close.position.end.offset
  if (lineText[openAt - lineStart] !== '(') return Option.none()
  const closeRel = lineText.indexOf(')', openAt - lineStart)
  if (closeRel < 0) return Option.none()
  const closeAt = lineStart + closeRel
  const inner = lineText.slice(openAt + 1 - lineStart, closeAt - lineStart)
  return Option.some({
    target: nodeOf(WarpAnchorTargetTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, openAt, closeAt + 1),
      source: lineText.slice(openAt - lineStart, closeAt + 1 - lineStart),
      health: okHealth,
      value: inner.trim(),
    }),
    end: closeAt + 1,
  })
}

const buildWarpAnchor = (
  open: AnchorOpenToken,
  close: AnchorCloseToken,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): { anchor: WarpAnchorToken; extras: ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken> } => {
  const line = linePosition.start.line
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  const contentStart = open.position.end.offset
  const contentEnd = close.position.start.offset
  const content = lineText.slice(contentStart - lineStart, contentEnd - lineStart)
  const { value, start, end } = trimSpan(content, contentStart)
  const namePos = span(line, start, end)
  const target = anchorTargetAt(close, lineText, linePosition)
  const afterTarget = Option.match(target, {
    onNone: () => close.position.end.offset,
    onSome: (t) => t.end,
  })
  const spec = anchorSpecifierAt(afterTarget, lineText, linePosition)
  const anchorEnd = Option.match(spec, { onNone: () => afterTarget, onSome: (s) => s.end })
  const anchorSource = lineText.slice(open.position.start.offset - lineStart, anchorEnd - lineStart)
  const targetTok = Option.getOrUndefined(Option.map(target, (t) => t.target))
  const specifier = Option.getOrUndefined(Option.map(spec, (s) => s.specifier))

  return pipe(
    decodeWarpAnchorName({
      type: 'WarpAnchorName',
      position: namePos,
      source: value,
      health: okHealth,
      value,
    }),
    Result.match({
      onFailure: () => ({
        anchor: assembleAnchor(
          open,
          close,
          nodeOf(WarpAnchorNameTokenSchema, {
            position: namePos,
            source: value,
            health: brokenLabel('anchorName', value, namePos),
            value: '',
          }),
          line,
          anchorSource,
          anchorEnd,
          targetTok,
          specifier,
        ),
        extras: unexpectedIfNonEmpty(namePos, value),
      }),
      onSuccess: (name) => ({
        anchor: assembleAnchor(
          open,
          close,
          name,
          line,
          anchorSource,
          anchorEnd,
          targetTok,
          specifier,
        ),
        extras: [],
      }),
    }),
  )
}

const assembleAnchor = (
  open: AnchorOpenToken,
  close: AnchorCloseToken,
  name: WarpAnchorNameToken,
  line: number,
  source: string,
  end: number,
  target: WarpAnchorTargetToken | undefined,
  specifier: Braces | undefined,
): WarpAnchorToken => {
  const status = aggregateStatus([
    open.health.status,
    name.health.status,
    close.health.status,
    ...(target ? [target.health.status] : []),
    ...(specifier ? [specifier.health.status] : []),
  ])
  return nodeOf(WarpAnchorTokenSchema, {
    position: span(line, open.position.start.offset, end),
    source,
    health: { status, diagnostics: [] },
    open,
    name,
    close,
    target,
    specifier,
  })
}

## Braces after an anchor

Braces after an anchor hold a label, as `::[Greeting function]{To be settled}` does, or parameters, as `::[Greeting function]{kind="To be settled" provides=greet}` does. The anchor and the braces that touch it form one directive, `::[…]{…}`, in code as in prose. An anchor is never the code's own syntax, so the braces that touch it are never the code's either. Code that needs a brace right after a name the anchor composes leaves a space between them, as in `::[Point] {X: 1}`, which Go, C++, and Rust all accept.

`bracesAt` reads the braces. `itemsFrom` reads what they hold as a run of items, each a word of a label or a parameter, up to the closing brace. Braces that hold anything else, such as `X: 1`, or that hold nothing, still read as a label, through `labelBracesAt`, so the label's fault names what is wrong. `braceTokens` gives the opening and closing brace their tokens.

The braces of a box and of a heading follow the same grammar, as the sections "A box line" and "Scanning a heading" describe, and `bracesAt` reads all three alike. The tokeniser reads the shape of braces and judges nothing they hold. Which keys and kinds a bearer of braces takes, and which key a label alone stands for, are for the passes to say, as the chapter "Small passes over the tree" describes.

=>

type Span = { readonly start: number; readonly end: number; readonly value: string }

type BraceItem =
  | { readonly type: 'word'; readonly word: Span }
  | {
      readonly type: 'parameter'
      readonly whole: Span
      readonly key: Span
      readonly values: ReadonlyArray<Span>
    }

const bracesAt = (
  openAt: number,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): Option.Option<{ specifier: Braces; end: number }> => {
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  return pipe(
    itemsFrom(lineText, openAt - lineStart + 1, []),
    Option.flatMap(({ items, closeAt }) =>
      Option.map(
        specifierOf(openAt, lineStart + closeAt, items, lineText, linePosition),
        (specifier) => ({ specifier, end: lineStart + closeAt + 1 }),
      ),
    ),
    Option.orElse(() => labelBracesAt(openAt, lineText, linePosition)),
  )
}

const isWord = (item: BraceItem): boolean => item.type === 'word'

const wordOf = (item: BraceItem): Option.Option<Span> =>
  item.type === 'word' ? Option.some(item.word) : Option.none()

const braceTokens = (
  openAt: number,
  closeAt: number,
  line: number,
): { open: SpecifierOpenToken; close: SpecifierCloseToken } => ({
  open: nodeOf(SpecifierOpenTokenSchema, {
    position: span(line, openAt, openAt + 1),
    source: '{',
    health: okHealth,
    value: '{',
  }),
  close: nodeOf(SpecifierCloseTokenSchema, {
    position: span(line, closeAt, closeAt + 1),
    source: '}',
    health: okHealth,
    value: '}',
  }),
})

const labelBracesAt = (
  openAt: number,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): Option.Option<{ specifier: Braces; end: number }> => {
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  const closeRel = lineText.indexOf('}', openAt - lineStart)
  if (closeRel < 0) return Option.none()
  const closeAt = lineStart + closeRel
  const { open, close } = braceTokens(openAt, closeAt, linePosition.start.line)
  return Option.map(
    constructSpecifier([open], [close], lineText, linePosition).token,
    (specifier) => ({ specifier, end: closeAt + 1 }),
  )
}

~

What the braces hold reads item by item. A word is a run of letters, digits, hyphens, and underscores. A parameter's key is a name that an equals sign follows, and its value is quoted, bare, or a list. The function `keyAt` reads a key with its equals sign, and gives the place where the value begins. The function `valueAt` reads a quoted value and undoes its escapes, or it reads a bare value of letters, digits, hyphens, underscores, and full stops. `listAt` reads values in square brackets, separated by commas. An item ends at a space or at the closing brace, so `provides=greet,farewell` is no run of items, and its braces read as a malformed label. A `Span` keeps where each piece starts and ends in the line, beside the value it means. `matchAt` matches a pattern at one place in a line, `skipSpaces` steps past the spaces there, and `endOf` gives the place where an item ends. `listContinued` reads the mark that follows a value: a comma goes on to the next value, a closing bracket ends the list, and anything else is no list.

=>

const matchAt = (pattern: RegExp, text: string, at: number): Option.Option<RegExpMatchArray> =>
  Option.fromNullishOr(text.slice(at).match(pattern))

const skipSpaces = (text: string, at: number): number => at + text.slice(at).search(/[^ ]|$/)

const spanOf = (text: string, start: number, end: number): Span => ({
  start,
  end,
  value: text.slice(start, end),
})

const endOf: (item: BraceItem) => number = byType<BraceItem>()({
  word: ({ word }) => word.end,
  parameter: ({ whole }) => whole.end,
})

const itemsFrom = (
  text: string,
  at: number,
  found: ReadonlyArray<BraceItem>,
): Option.Option<{ items: ReadonlyArray<BraceItem>; closeAt: number }> => {
  const next = skipSpaces(text, at)
  return text[next] === '}'
    ? Option.filter(Option.some({ items: found, closeAt: next }), () => found.length > 0)
    : pipe(
        Option.orElse(parameterAt(text, next), () => wordAt(text, next)),
        Option.filter((item) => text[endOf(item)] === ' ' || text[endOf(item)] === '}'),
        Option.flatMap((item) => itemsFrom(text, endOf(item), [...found, item])),
      )
}

const wordAt = (text: string, at: number): Option.Option<BraceItem> =>
  Option.map(matchAt(/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+/, text, at), (match): BraceItem => ({
    type: 'word',
    word: spanOf(text, at, at + match[0].length),
  }))

const valueAt = (text: string, at: number): Option.Option<Span> =>
  Option.orElse(
    Option.map(matchAt(/^"((?:[^"\\]|\\.)*)"/, text, at), (match) => ({
      start: at,
      end: at + match[0].length,
      value: (match[1] ?? '').replace(/\\(.)/g, '$1'),
    })),
    () =>
      Option.map(matchAt(/^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+/, text, at), (match) =>
        spanOf(text, at, at + match[0].length),
      ),
  )

const listAt = (
  text: string,
  at: number,
): Option.Option<{ values: ReadonlyArray<Span>; end: number }> =>
  text[at] === '[' ? listFrom(text, skipSpaces(text, at + 1), []) : Option.none()

const listContinued = Match.fn(
  (
    _within: {
      readonly text: string
      readonly next: number
      readonly found: ReadonlyArray<Span>
      readonly value: Span
    },
    mark: string | undefined,
  ) => mark,
).pipe(
  Match.withReturnType<Option.Option<{ values: ReadonlyArray<Span>; end: number }>>(),
  Match.when(',', (_mark, { text, next, found, value }) =>
    listFrom(text, skipSpaces(text, next + 1), [...found, value]),
  ),
  Match.when(']', (_mark, { next, found, value }) =>
    Option.some({ values: [...found, value], end: next + 1 }),
  ),
  Match.orElse(() => Option.none()),
)

const listFrom = (
  text: string,
  at: number,
  found: ReadonlyArray<Span>,
): Option.Option<{ values: ReadonlyArray<Span>; end: number }> =>
  Option.flatMap(valueAt(text, at), (value) => {
    const next = skipSpaces(text, value.end)
    return listContinued({ text, next, found, value }, text[next])
  })

const keyAt = (text: string, at: number): Option.Option<{ key: Span; from: number }> =>
  Option.map(matchAt(/^([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*)=/, text, at), (match) => ({
    key: spanOf(text, at, at + match[0].length - 1),
    from: at + match[0].length,
  }))

const parameterAt = (text: string, at: number): Option.Option<BraceItem> =>
  Option.flatMap(keyAt(text, at), ({ key, from }) =>
    Option.map(
      Option.orElse(listAt(text, from), () =>
        Option.map(valueAt(text, from), (value) => ({ values: [value], end: value.end })),
      ),
      ({ values, end }): BraceItem => ({
        type: 'parameter',
        whole: spanOf(text, at, end),
        key,
        values,
      }),
    ),
  )

~

Braces whose items are all words hold a label, which `specifierOf` builds through `constructSpecifier`. Braces with a parameter among their items hold `Parameters`, which `parametersOf` builds, and `parameterToken` builds each parameter.

A label may not stand beside parameters, but an author may write one there. `strayLabels` keeps each run of words beside the parameters as one label on the token, so the pass that expands short forms can fault it where it stands. `Array.groupWith` gathers consecutive items of one type, so words that a parameter separates form two runs.

=>

const specifierOf = (
  openAt: number,
  closeAt: number,
  items: ReadonlyArray<BraceItem>,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): Option.Option<Braces> => {
  const { open, close } = braceTokens(openAt, closeAt, linePosition.start.line)
  return items.every(isWord)
    ? constructSpecifier([open], [close], lineText, linePosition).token
    : Option.some(parametersOf(open, close, items, lineText, linePosition))
}

const parametersOf = (
  open: SpecifierOpenToken,
  close: SpecifierCloseToken,
  items: ReadonlyArray<BraceItem>,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): ParametersToken => {
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  const at = (piece: Span): Position =>
    span(linePosition.start.line, lineStart + piece.start, lineStart + piece.end)
  const position = span(
    linePosition.start.line,
    open.position.start.offset,
    close.position.end.offset,
  )
  return nodeOf(ParametersTokenSchema, {
    position,
    source: lineText.slice(position.start.offset - lineStart, position.end.offset - lineStart),
    health: okHealth,
    open,
    parameters: Array.getSomes(items.map(parameterOf)).map((parameter) =>
      parameterToken(parameter, lineText, at),
    ),
    labels: strayLabels(items, at),
    close,
  })
}

type WrittenParameter = Extract<BraceItem, { type: 'parameter' }>

const parameterOf = (item: BraceItem): Option.Option<WrittenParameter> =>
  item.type === 'parameter' ? Option.some(item) : Option.none()

const parameterToken = (
  parameter: WrittenParameter,
  lineText: string,
  at: (piece: Span) => Position,
): ParameterToken =>
  nodeOf(ParameterTokenSchema, {
    position: at(parameter.whole),
    source: parameter.whole.value,
    health: okHealth,
    key: nodeOf(ParameterKeyTokenSchema, {
      position: at(parameter.key),
      source: parameter.key.value,
      health: okHealth,
      value: parameter.key.value,
    }),
    values: parameter.values.map((value) =>
      nodeOf(ParameterValueTokenSchema, {
        position: at(value),
        source: lineText.slice(value.start, value.end),
        health: okHealth,
        value: value.value,
      }),
    ),
  })

const strayLabels = (
  items: ReadonlyArray<BraceItem>,
  at: (piece: Span) => Position,
): ReadonlyArray<SpecifierLabelToken> =>
  Array.match(items, {
    onEmpty: (): ReadonlyArray<SpecifierLabelToken> => [],
    onNonEmpty: (all) =>
      Array.groupWith(all, (one, other) => one.type === other.type)
        .map((run) => Array.getSomes(run.map(wordOf)))
        .flatMap((run) =>
          Array.match(run, {
            onEmpty: (): ReadonlyArray<SpecifierLabelToken> => [],
            onNonEmpty: (words) => [
              buildSpecifierLabel(words.map((word) => word.value).join(' '), {
                start: at(Array.headNonEmpty(words)).start,
                end: at(Array.lastNonEmpty(words)).end,
              }),
            ],
          }),
        ),
  })

## Braces an author is still writing

An author writes braces one letter at a time, and the editor offers what may stand at the cursor: a label, a key, or a value. The tokens cannot say which. Braces that are half written read as something else, so `{kind=` is a malformed label, and a brace that no closing brace follows forms no token at all after an anchor. So the function `slotAt` reads the text itself, from the brace to the cursor, with the readers of items that `bracesAt` uses. The offer and the tokeniser then agree on what a word, a key, and a value are.

A `BraceSlot` is the place a choice fills. It names the `brace` that opens the braces, the `start` and `end` of the text the choice replaces, and whether a brace closes them. It also carries the `kind` the braces name under their `kind` key, when they name one. A bearer's kind decides which keys it takes, and half-written braces give their bearer no token to read the kind from. Its `wants` field says what stands there, and it holds one of three.

- **A label.** The braces hold words and no parameter, so a label may stand there, or the first key. The slot covers everything between the braces, or runs to the cursor when no brace closes them.
- **A key.** A parameter stands elsewhere in the braces, so only a key may follow. The slot covers the word under the cursor, and `written` lists the keys the braces hold already, since a key written twice is a fault.
- **A value.** A key and its equals sign stand before the cursor. The slot covers the value under the cursor, and names its `key`. In a list it covers the one item under the cursor. When a quote opens the value, the slot starts after the quote and is `quoted`, so a choice is written between the quotes as it is.

The function `slotAt` cuts the cursor's line out of the text, and `braceBefore` finds the nearest opening brace before the cursor that no closing brace follows. From that brace, `slotIn` reads the line. First `itemsBefore` reads each item that a space ends before the cursor, so what remains is the piece the author is writing. When that piece opens with a key, `valueSlot` reads the slot of its value, and `wordSlot` reads every other piece as a label or a key. Four small readers find the places a slot names: `closeAfter` the brace that closes the braces, `itemStart` the start of an item in a list, and `runEnd` and `quoteEnd` the end of the text under the cursor. The items around the cursor are the ones before it and the ones `itemsAfter` reads from where the slot ends, up to the closing brace. The function `keysIn` lists the keys those items write, and `kindIn` reads the kind they name. A search gives its index as a number, and `whereFound` turns that number into an `Option`.

=>

export type Wanted =
  | { readonly type: 'label' }
  | { readonly type: 'key'; readonly written: ReadonlyArray<string> }
  | { readonly type: 'value'; readonly key: string; readonly quoted: boolean }

export type BraceSlot = {
  readonly brace: number
  readonly start: number
  readonly end: number
  readonly closed: boolean
  readonly kind: Option.Option<string>
  readonly wants: Wanted
}

const whereFound = (index: number): Option.Option<number> =>
  index < 0 ? Option.none() : Option.some(index)

const braceBefore = (line: string, cursor: number): Option.Option<number> =>
  Option.filter(
    whereFound(line.slice(0, cursor).lastIndexOf('{')),
    (brace) => !line.slice(brace, cursor).includes('}'),
  )

const closeAfter = (line: string, cursor: number): Option.Option<number> =>
  Option.filter(
    whereFound(line.indexOf('}', cursor)),
    (close) => !line.slice(cursor, close).includes('{'),
  )

type Before = { readonly items: ReadonlyArray<BraceItem>; readonly at: number }

const itemsBefore = (
  line: string,
  at: number,
  cursor: number,
  found: ReadonlyArray<BraceItem>,
): Before => {
  const next = Math.min(skipSpaces(line, at), cursor)
  return pipe(
    Option.orElse(parameterAt(line, next), () => wordAt(line, next)),
    Option.filter((item) => endOf(item) < cursor && line[endOf(item)] === ' '),
    Option.match({
      onNone: (): Before => ({ items: found, at: next }),
      onSome: (item) => itemsBefore(line, endOf(item), cursor, [...found, item]),
    }),
  )
}

const runEnd = (pattern: RegExp, line: string, cursor: number): number =>
  Option.match(matchAt(pattern, line, cursor), {
    onNone: () => cursor,
    onSome: (match) => cursor + match[0].length,
  })

const quoteEnd = (line: string, cursor: number): number =>
  Option.getOrElse(whereFound(line.indexOf('"', cursor)), () => cursor)

const itemStart = (line: string, from: number, cursor: number): number => {
  const typed = line.slice(from, cursor)
  const item = typed.startsWith('[')
    ? from + Math.max(typed.lastIndexOf(','), typed.lastIndexOf('[')) + 1
    : from
  return Math.min(skipSpaces(line, item), cursor)
}

type Within = {
  readonly line: string
  readonly brace: number
  readonly cursor: number
  readonly before: Before
  readonly close: Option.Option<number>
}

const itemsAfter = (line: string, end: number): ReadonlyArray<BraceItem> =>
  Option.match(itemsFrom(line, end, []), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<BraceItem> => [],
    onSome: ({ items }) => items,
  })

const keysIn = (items: ReadonlyArray<BraceItem>): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  Array.getSomes(items.map(parameterOf)).map((parameter) => parameter.key.value)

const kindIn = (items: ReadonlyArray<BraceItem>): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Array.findFirst(Array.getSomes(items.map(parameterOf)), ({ key }) => key.value === 'kind'),
    Option.flatMap((parameter) => Array.head(parameter.values)),
    Option.map((value) => value.value),
  )

const valueSlot = (
  { line, brace, cursor, before, close }: Within,
  key: string,
  from: number,
): BraceSlot => {
  const item = itemStart(line, from, cursor)
  const quoted = item < cursor && line[item] === '"'
  const end = quoted ? quoteEnd(line, cursor) : runEnd(/^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]*/, line, cursor)
  return {
    brace,
    start: quoted ? item + 1 : item,
    end,
    closed: Option.isSome(close),
    kind: kindIn([...before.items, ...itemsAfter(line, end)]),
    wants: { type: 'value', key, quoted },
  }
}

const wordSlot = ({ line, brace, cursor, before, close }: Within): BraceSlot => {
  const end = runEnd(/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]*/, line, cursor)
  const around = [...before.items, ...itemsAfter(line, end)]
  const written = keysIn(around)
  const closed = Option.isSome(close)
  return written.length === 0
    ? {
        brace,
        start: brace + 1,
        end: Option.getOrElse(close, () => cursor),
        closed,
        kind: Option.none(),
        wants: { type: 'label' },
      }
    : {
        brace,
        start: before.at,
        end,
        closed,
        kind: kindIn(around),
        wants: { type: 'key', written },
      }
}

const slotIn = (line: string, brace: number, cursor: number): BraceSlot => {
  const before = itemsBefore(line, brace + 1, cursor, [])
  const within: Within = { line, brace, cursor, before, close: closeAfter(line, cursor) }
  return pipe(
    keyAt(line, before.at),
    Option.filter(({ from }) => from <= cursor),
    Option.match({
      onNone: () => wordSlot(within),
      onSome: ({ key, from }) => valueSlot(within, key.value, from),
    }),
  )
}

export const slotAt = (text: string, offset: number): Option.Option<BraceSlot> => {
  const lineStart = text.slice(0, offset).lastIndexOf('\n') + 1
  const lineEnd = Option.getOrElse(whereFound(text.indexOf('\n', offset)), () => text.length)
  const line = text.slice(lineStart, lineEnd)
  return Option.map(braceBefore(line, offset - lineStart), (brace) => {
    const slot = slotIn(line, brace, offset - lineStart)
    return {
      ...slot,
      brace: lineStart + slot.brace,
      start: lineStart + slot.start,
      end: lineStart + slot.end,
    }
  })
}

## A box line

A line that the classifier marked as a box line holds no prose, so `tokeniseBoxLine` settles its `Box` token in place of the anchors and warps that a prose line holds. `settledBox` settles the token by its side. A line that closes a box holds nothing past its colons, so its token is whole as it stands.

A line that opens a box may hold a name and braces after its colons. The braces come last, so `trailingBracesAt` finds a pair of braces that ends the line, and `bracesAt` reads what they hold. Braces anywhere else on the line belong to the name, so `::: Why {x} matters` names a box `Why {x} matters`. The name is the text between the colons and the braces, and `boxNameAt` trims it. A line that holds only colons and braces opens a box with no name, and a line of colons alone opens a note with no name.

=>

const tokeniseBoxLine = <Line extends PreambleWeft | ProseWeft>(
  text: string,
  weft: Line,
  box: BoxToken,
): Line => {
  const settled = settledBox(text, weft.position, box)
  return {
    ...weft,
    source: lineSlice(text, weft.position),
    health: { status: settled.health.status, diagnostics: [] },
    box: settled,
  }
}

const settledBox = Match.fn(
  (_text: string, _linePosition: Position, box: BoxToken) => box.side,
).pipe(
  Match.withReturnType<BoxToken>(),
  Match.when('open', (_side, text, linePosition, box) => openedBox(text, linePosition, box)),
  Match.when('close', (_side, _text, _linePosition, box) =>
    nodeOf(BoxTokenSchema, { ...box, health: okHealth }),
  ),
  Match.when('stray', (_side, _text, _linePosition, box) => strayBox(box)),
  Match.exhaustive,
)

const trailingBraces = /\{[^{}]*\}[ \t]*$/

const trailingBracesAt = (content: string, from: number): Option.Option<number> =>
  Option.map(
    Option.fromNullishOr(trailingBraces.exec(content.slice(from))),
    (match) => from + match.index,
  )

const boxNameAt = (raw: string, rawStart: number, line: number): Option.Option<BoxNameToken> => {
  const { value, start, end } = trimSpan(raw, rawStart)
  return value === ''
    ? Option.none()
    : Option.some(
        nodeOf(BoxNameTokenSchema, {
          position: span(line, start, end),
          source: value,
          health: okHealth,
          value,
        }),
      )
}

const openedBox = (text: string, linePosition: Position, box: BoxToken): BoxToken => {
  const lineText = lineSlice(text, linePosition)
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  const content = lineText.replace(/\r?\n$/, '')
  const from = box.fence.position.end.offset - lineStart
  const braces = Option.flatMap(trailingBracesAt(content, from), (openAt) =>
    bracesAt(lineStart + openAt, lineText, linePosition),
  )
  const specifier = Option.map(braces, (held) => held.specifier)
  const nameEnd = Option.match(specifier, {
    onNone: () => content.length,
    onSome: (held) => held.position.start.offset - lineStart,
  })
  const name = boxNameAt(content.slice(from, nameEnd), lineStart + from, linePosition.start.line)
  return nodeOf(BoxTokenSchema, {
    ...box,
    health: {
      status: aggregateStatus([
        box.fence.health.status,
        ...Option.toArray(specifier).map((held) => held.health.status),
      ]),
      diagnostics: [],
    },
    name: Option.getOrUndefined(name),
    specifier: Option.getOrUndefined(specifier),
  })
}

~

A stray opens and closes nothing, and `strayBox` faults its colons. A run of four or more colons is a `ColonRun` fault, whose fix writes three colons in its place. Three colons that would open a box inside an open one are a `NestedBox` fault. Both are errors, so a tangle refuses the loom rather than guess where its author meant a box to end.

=>

const strayBox = (box: BoxToken): BoxToken => {
  const health = faulty(
    box.fence.source.length > 3 ? ColonRun({ colons: box.fence.source }) : NestedBox(),
    box.fence.position,
  )
  return nodeOf(BoxTokenSchema, {
    ...box,
    health: { status: health.status, diagnostics: [] },
    fence: nodeOf(BoxFenceTokenSchema, { ...box.fence, health }),
  })
}

## Scanning a line for warps and anchors

`constructWarps` scans a line, pairs the `{{`/`}}` delimiters, and builds a warp per pair. `constructAnchors` scans a line for `::[` opens and builds an anchor for each, closing it on the first `]` that follows. Both take a scan text apart from the line itself, so a prose handler passes the line with its Markdown code spans blanked — a token inside backticks is found nowhere, while the one outside still reads its content from the original line. There is no fallback: a token is always a token. A malformed anchor — unclosed, or empty-named — is kept as an anchor carrying error health, never reinterpreted as literal code; an anchor whose name does not resolve likewise stays an anchor. Each is a diagnostic Loom reports, and a hard failure at tangle. The rare literal `::[` in product code is written through the host language's character escape, so the tokeniser never has to guess whether a `::[` was meant.

=>

const constructWarps = (
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
  scanText: string = lineText,
): {
  tokens: ReadonlyArray<WarpToken>
  unexpected: ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken>
} => {
  const opens = scanWarpOpen(scanText, linePosition)
  const closes = scanWarpClose(scanText, linePosition)
  const { pairs, stray } = pairWarpDelims(opens, closes, scanText, linePosition)
  const tokens = pairs.map(({ open, close }) => buildWarp(open, close, lineText, linePosition))
  return { tokens, unexpected: stray }
}

const indicesOf = (text: string, needle: string): ReadonlyArray<number> => {
  const step = (found: ReadonlyArray<number>, from: number): ReadonlyArray<number> => {
    const at = text.indexOf(needle, from)
    return at < 0 ? found : step([...found, at], at + needle.length)
  }
  return step([], 0)
}

const scanAnchorOpens = (
  open: string,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): ReadonlyArray<AnchorOpenToken> => {
  const line = linePosition.start.line
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  return indicesOf(lineText, open).map((i) =>
    nodeOf(AnchorOpenTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, lineStart + i, lineStart + i + open.length),
      source: open,
      health: okHealth,
      value: open,
    }),
  )
}

const anchorClose = (
  open: AnchorOpenToken,
  close: string,
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
): AnchorCloseToken => {
  const line = linePosition.start.line
  const lineStart = linePosition.start.offset
  const lineEnd = contentEnd(lineText, lineStart)
  const rel = lineText.indexOf(close, open.position.end.offset - lineStart)
  return rel < 0
    ? nodeOf(AnchorCloseTokenSchema, {
        position: span(line, lineEnd, lineEnd),
        source: '',
        health: missingClosing(line, open.position.start.offset, lineEnd, close),
        value: close,
      })
    : nodeOf(AnchorCloseTokenSchema, {
        position: span(line, lineStart + rel, lineStart + rel + close.length),
        source: close,
        health: okHealth,
        value: close,
      })
}

const constructAnchors = (
  lineText: string,
  linePosition: Position,
  delims: AnchorDelims,
  scanText: string = lineText,
): {
  tokens: ReadonlyArray<WarpAnchorToken>
  unexpected: ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken>
} => {
  const built = scanAnchorOpens(delims.open, scanText, linePosition).map((open) =>
    buildWarpAnchor(
      open,
      anchorClose(open, delims.close, scanText, linePosition),
      lineText,
      linePosition,
    ),
  )
  return {
    tokens: built.map((b) => b.anchor),
    unexpected: built.flatMap((b) => b.extras),
  }
}

## The heading title

`headingTitle` is the text between the heading marker and the first structural token, trimmed. It returns nothing when that span is empty, and text after the first structural token is dropped.

=>

const headingTitle = (
  text: string,
  position: Position,
  headingStartEnd: number,
  consumed: ReadonlyArray<Position>,
): Option.Option<HeadingTitleToken> => {
  const line = position.start.line
  const lineText = text.slice(position.start.offset, position.end.offset)
  const trailingWs = lineText.match(/\s*$/)?.[0].length ?? 0

  const titleEnd = consumed
    .map((p) => p.start.offset)
    .filter((offset) => offset >= headingStartEnd)
    .reduce((min, offset) => Math.min(min, offset), position.end.offset - trailingWs)

  const raw = text.slice(headingStartEnd, titleEnd)
  const leading = raw.match(/^\s*/)?.[0].length ?? 0
  const trailing = raw.match(/\s*$/)?.[0].length ?? 0
  const start = headingStartEnd + leading
  const end = titleEnd - trailing
  if (start >= end) return Option.none()

  return Option.some(
    nodeOf(HeadingTitleTokenSchema, {
      position: span(line, start, end),
      source: text.slice(start, end),
      health: okHealth,
    }),
  )
}

## Scanning a heading

`scanHeading` builds a heading's subnodes, the specifier, the sink, and the title, from one line. It records the spans they consume, so the title knows where to stop.

A heading's specifier opens at the first brace on its line. `headingSpecifier` reads it through `bracesAt`, so a heading holds a label or parameters, as an anchor and a box do. Every brace outside the span that `bracesAt` reads stays an unexpected token, which `strayBraces` finds, so `## Greeting {Bash} {Tangle}` faults its second pair of braces. A brace that no closing brace follows reads as a label through `constructSpecifier`, whose missing close reports `expected closing` once.

A heading's sink is a file's path in square brackets, as `## Saying hello [hello.ts]` writes it. A list in the braces stands in square brackets too, as `{kind=Task tags=[walls, paint]}` holds one, and those brackets belong to the list, not to a sink. So the sink reads only the brackets that stand outside the braces, which `outsideBraces` keeps.

=>

type HeadingTokens = {
  readonly specifier: Option.Option<Braces>
  readonly sink: Option.Option<SinkToken>
  readonly title: Option.Option<HeadingTitleToken>
  readonly unexpected: ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken>
}

const strayBraces = (
  braces: ReadonlyArray<Positioned>,
  from: number,
  to: number,
): ReadonlyArray<UnexpectedToken> =>
  braces
    .filter((brace) => brace.position.start.offset < from || brace.position.start.offset >= to)
    .map(toUnexpected)

const headingSpecifier = (lineText: string, position: Position): Construction<Braces> => {
  const opens = scanSpecifierOpen(lineText, position)
  const closes = scanSpecifierClose(lineText, position)
  return pipe(
    Array.head(opens),
    Option.flatMap((open) =>
      Option.map(
        bracesAt(open.position.start.offset, lineText, position),
        ({ specifier, end }): Construction<Braces> => ({
          token: Option.some(specifier),
          unexpected: strayBraces([...opens, ...closes], open.position.start.offset, end),
        }),
      ),
    ),
    Option.getOrElse((): Construction<Braces> =>
      constructSpecifier(opens, closes, lineText, position),
    ),
  )
}

const outsideBraces =
  (braces: Option.Option<Braces>) =>
  (bracket: { readonly position: Position }): boolean =>
    !Option.exists(
      braces,
      (held) =>
        bracket.position.start.offset >= held.position.start.offset &&
        bracket.position.start.offset < held.position.end.offset,
    )

const scanHeading = (text: string, position: Position, headingStartEnd: number): HeadingTokens => {
  const lineText = text.slice(position.start.offset, position.end.offset)

  const specResult = headingSpecifier(lineText, position)
  const outside = outsideBraces(specResult.token)
  const sinkResult = constructSink(
    scanSinkOpen(lineText, position).filter(outside),
    scanSinkClose(lineText, position).filter(outside),
    lineText,
    position,
  )

  const unexpected = [...specResult.unexpected, ...sinkResult.unexpected]

  const consumed: ReadonlyArray<Position> = [
    ...Option.toArray(specResult.token).map((s) => s.position),
    ...Option.toArray(sinkResult.token).map((s) => s.position),
    ...unexpected.map((u) => u.position),
  ]
  const title = headingTitle(text, position, headingStartEnd, consumed)

  return {
    specifier: specResult.token,
    sink: sinkResult.token,
    title,
    unexpected,
  }
}

## Tokenising a heading

`tokeniseHeading` assembles the heading weft from its scanned subnodes — the title, the specifier, and the sink — and aggregates their health. The title names the section: `normaliseTitle` turns it into an identifier — `What the compiler draws on` becomes `WhatTheCompilerDrawsOn` — which the product pass reads to identify the section. Naming makes no name unique; two titles that normalise alike are a duplicate the diagnostics mark.

=>

export const normaliseTitle = (title: string): string => {
  const pascal = (title.match(/[A-Za-z0-9]+/g) ?? [])
    .map((w) => w.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + w.slice(1))
    .join('')
  return /^[A-Za-z]/.test(pascal) ? pascal : `_${pascal}`
}

const tokeniseHeading = (text: string, weft: HeadingWeft): LoomWeft => {
  const { specifier, sink, title, unexpected } = scanHeading(
    text,
    weft.position,
    weft.headingStart.position.end.offset,
  )

  const status = aggregateStatus([
    weft.headingStart.health.status,
    ...Option.toArray(specifier).map((s) => s.health.status),
    ...Option.toArray(sink).map((s) => s.health.status),
    ...Option.toArray(title).map((t) => t.health.status),
    ...unexpected.map(() => 'error' as const),
  ])

  return nodeOf(HeadingWeftSchema, {
    position: weft.position,
    source: lineSlice(text, weft.position),
    health: strayHealth(text, status, unexpected),
    unexpected: unexpected.length > 0 ? unexpected : undefined,
    headingStart: weft.headingStart,
    title: Option.getOrUndefined(title),
    specifier: Option.getOrUndefined(specifier),
    sink: Option.getOrUndefined(sink),
  })
}

## What this module draws on

Effect supplies `Array.mapAccum` for the fold over the wefts, `Match` for the smaller choices inside a weft, `Schema` for decoding, and the rest. `byType`, from the node foundation, sends each weft to the tokeniser of its kind. The token schemas and `getProbe` come from `LoomTokens`, with the keys and kinds that the braces of an anchor and of a box are checked against, the weft schemas and `nextBox` from `Weft`, and the health and `UnexpectedToken` types from the foundation, with `nodeOf`, which constructs every token, and the `NodeSchema` it constructs one of. `LoomFault` gives `faulty` and the fault constructors each diagnostic here is built from.

=>

